<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Belgian Congo</h1><p class="st-meta">1908 CE – 1960 CE · capital Boma; later Léopoldville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Belgium's vast central African colony, a paternalistic mining economy whose hasty independence in 1960 led to the Congo Crisis.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After international outrage over abuses in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, the Belgian parliament annexed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II's</a> personal domain on 15 November 1908. A Colonial Charter placed it under the Belgian government, with a governor-general at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boma">Boma</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Rule rested on the state, the Catholic missions and large companies, the so-called colonial trinity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-miniere-du-haut-katanga">Union Minière du Haut Katanga</a> mined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and cobalt, and uranium from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinkolobwe">Shinkolobwe</a> supplied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a>. Primary schooling and health care spread widely, but Africans were barred from higher posts and university until the 1950s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-kimbangu">Simon Kimbangu's</a> prophetic church was repressed. The capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Léopoldville</a> in the 1920s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Léopoldville</a> in 1959 led <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Belgium</a> to grant independence on 30 June 1960, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-kasavubu">Joseph Kasavubu</a> as president and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a> as prime minister. Mutiny and the secession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> followed within days.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/belgian-congo.jpg" alt="Belgian Congo" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: See file history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Congo_Free_State.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1908 CE – 1960 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Boma; later Léopoldville</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Albert I, Baudouin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism, Protestantism, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Lingala, Swahili, Kikongo, Tshiluba</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Congo Belge</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1934&amp;focus=belgian-congo" aria-label="Belgian Congo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5042 2396 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Belgian Congo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5362 2652L5362 2652M5362 2652L5099 670"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5362 2652h0M5099 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5362 2652h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5362 2652h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Boma. <a href="/atlas?year=1934&amp;focus=belgian-congo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
